<TopicPage slug="subjects/film/expert/film-theory-and-criticism">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Film theory is the <strong>literature lesson's theory, in motion</strong> — realism vs
    formalism, the gaze, genre contracts, authorship — plus the questions film uniquely forces:
    what does the machine itself do to the watching mind? This is the ladder's summit: you can
    no longer watch a film naively, which is the point. The syllabus binds the lesson to the
    documentary unit, and the binding is deliberate: documentary is where every theoretical
    question gets asked at maximum pressure — what is the film's relationship to the real, who
    is speaking about whom, and what does the camera <em>do</em> to the people in front of it.
    Theory, criticism and documentary turn out to be one conversation.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="apparatus, gaze, genre/authorship, the discipline now" widget="equationStep" config={theoryCfg} />

  <h2>Realism and formalism: the founding debate</h2>
  <p>
    The discipline's first argument defines its poles. <strong>Formalism</strong> — the Soviet
    school of the 1920s: Vertov's kino-eye (the camera as a machine seeing what humans cannot),
    Eisenstein's <strong>montage</strong> — held that meaning is <em>made</em> in the
    juxtaposition: shot A of a face plus shot B of a plate of soup produces "hunger", a meaning
    neither shot contains (the Kuleshov effect, demonstrated in the 1910s–20s and confirmed by
    modern eye-tracking and neuroscience). Eisenstein's Odessa Steps sequence in <em>Battleship
    Potemkin</em> (1925) — sixty-some shots compressing one massacre into escalating rhythm —
    is the manifesto demonstrated. <strong>Realism</strong> — André Bazin's <em>What Is
    Cinema?</em> (1958–62) — held the opposite: the camera's ontology is honesty, its
    uninterrupted recording of reality approaches a "change mummified", and the honest
    techniques are deep focus and the long take, which respect the spectator's freedom to look
    (his model cases: Welles's depth of field, Italian neorealism's non-professional actors in
    real streets). Neither camp was naive: Bazin championed montage-averse styles because he
    believed <em>ideology</em> lives in technique, and Eisenstein believed reality's meaning
    needed construction. The debate's modern settlement is generative rather than either/or:
    every style is an argument about the real, and close analysis asks which argument this
    film is making.
  </p>

  <h2>Documentary film: theory's hardest test case</h2>
  <p>
    Documentary is where realism's claims face their stress test, because its contract with
    the audience is explicit and constantly renegotiated. John Grierson, who coined the term
    (reviewing Flaherty's <em>Moana</em>, 1926), defined documentary as "the creative treatment
    of actuality" — and the definition's honesty is the point: creative <em>and</em> actual,
    always both. Flaherty's <em>Nanook of the North</em> (1922) staged its walrus hunt and
    rebuilt its igloo — a film about actuality, constructed. The theory's modern organiser is
    Bill Nichols' six <strong>modes</strong>: expository (voice-of-god narration — the 1930s
    March of Time tradition), observational (direct cinema: the Maysles brothers, Wiseman —
    camera as fly on the wall), participatory (the filmmaker enters the frame — Michael
    Moore's interviews), reflexive (the film shows its own making — Man with a Movie Camera's
    cameraman climbing bridges), performative (the filmmaker's own experience as evidence —
    personal documentary), and poetic (associative image-rhythm over argument). Each mode is a
    different answer to the same ethics: who speaks, and who is spoken for. The contemporary
    cases sharpen it: <em>The Act of Killing</em> (2012) had Indonesian death-squad leaders
    re-enact their own massacres as film genres — participatory, reflexive and performative at
    once, and one of the most argued documentaries of the century precisely because its mode
    choices carry moral weight.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "documentary means objective truth, unmediated." Every
    documentary is a set of choices — what to shoot, whom to interview, what order, what to
    leave on the cutting-room floor — and those choices are the authorship. The honest claim
    is not "this is reality" but "this is a faithful argument about reality", and the critic's
    job includes auditing the argument: whose voices are absent, which footage does the edit
    recontextualise, does the music editorialise? Documentary criticism is applied film
    theory, because the stakes are reputations, policies and justice — not just endings.
  </p>

  <h2>Critical debates: the apparatus, the gaze, and ideology</h2>
  <p>
    The 1970s turned theory toward the spectator. <strong>Apparatus theory</strong> (Baudry,
    Metz, drawing on Althusser and psychoanalysis) argued the cinema machine itself positions
    the viewer: darkened room, fixed seat, projected image — a technological arrangement that
    produces passivity and identification (ideology built into the seat). Laura Mulvey's
    "Visual Pleasure and Narrative Cinema" (1975) sharpened it into the most-cited essay in
    the field: classical Hollywood organises looking around the <strong>male gaze</strong> —
    women displayed as spectacle (to-be-looked-at-ness), men driving the action, with the
    camera, characters and audience aligned on the masculine position. The essay's influence
    is measurable: it reshaped criticism, film schools and eventually production (counter-
    cinemas, then mainstream corrections), and it seeded the whole industry of
    representation analysis — the Bechdel test (1985, a comic-strip joke that became a
    headline metric: two women, who talk to each other, about something other than a man) is
    its pop descendant. The debates stay live because the theory invites both use and abuse:
    over-application flattens films that complicate the scheme (Hitchcock's women are
    simultaneously objects and the films' intelligences), and later scholars (bell hooks'
    "oppositional gaze"; queer spectatorship) argued Mulvey's spectator was itself
    unmarked — male, white, straight by default. The discipline's mature position: the gaze
    is a hypothesis about structure, not a verdict about audiences — a lens to apply with
    evidence about how this film actually arranges looking.
  </p>

  <h2>Filmmakers' theories: the author and the manifesto</h2>
  <p>
    The auteur question is film theory's most consequential export to production. Truffaut's
    1954 <em>Cahiers du cinéma</em> essay ("a certain tendency in French cinema") attacked
    the tradition de qualité and proposed that the <strong>director is the author</strong>
    whose personal vision survives the studio system — the politique des auteurs, imported
    to America by Andrew Sarris (1962) as "auteur theory". Its evidence was stylistic
    authorship across factory conditions: Hitchcock's Suspicion-avoidance rules (the bomb
    under the table — suspense is giving the audience the information), Ford's composition,
    Ophüls's camera. The theory's critics (Pauline Kael's "Circles and Squares", 1963)
    answered that it mystifies collaboration (screenwriters, cinematographers, editors),
    canonises inconsistency, and flatters directors — all partly true, which is why the
    mature version is careful: auteurism is a <em>reading strategy</em> (look for the policy
    that unifies a body of work), not a fact about labour. The <strong>manifesto
    tradition</strong> makes filmmakers' theories explicit: Vertov's kino-eye writings
    (1920s), Eisenstein's montage essays, Truffaut and Godard's Cahiers polemics, Tarkovsky's
    <em>Sculpting in Time</em> (rhythm as the film's true material), and Dogme 95's "Vow of
    Chastity" (von Trier and Vinterberg, 1995: no artificial light, no props brought in, no
    genre movies — rules so strict they functioned as publicity and creative stimulus at
    once). The loop the film ladder keeps drawing closes here: today's grammar lesson is
    yesterday's theory essay.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "auteur theory means the director made the film
    single-handedly." It never did — it claims a <em>recognisable authorial intelligence</em>
    can be traced across a director's work, which is compatible with (and dependent on) mass
    collaboration. Deploying it as a credit-hoarding fact, rather than an interpretive lens,
    is the error Kael was pointing at in 1963.
  </p>

  <h2>Genre and the spectator's contract</h2>
  <p>
    Genre theory treats film categories as <strong>contracts</strong>: the western promises
    landscape, violence with meaning and the frontier's moral problem; the musical promises
    numbers; the horror film promises the machinery of dread. The contract mechanism explains
    how subversion works — <em>Unforgiven</em> (1992) spends the western's accumulated
    expectations to argue against the myth the genre built; knowing the contract is
    prerequisite to reading the violation, which is why genre films reward genre-literate
    audiences and why critics map lineage before judging novelty. The theoretical tools:
    genre as ritual (audiences renewing shared values — the 1950s western debate), genre as
    economic strategy (studios managing risk with recognisable products — the industrial
    lens), and genre as evolving system (Altman's semantics/syntax distinction: the western's
    semantics are hats and horses, its syntax is the civilisation/wilderness opposition —
    which is why <em>Star Wars</em> can be a western wearing spacecraft). Documentary
    intersects genre here too: the true-crime wave, the concert film, the sports documentary
    (the <em>30 for 30</em> format) are genres with their own contracts — the sophisticated
    spectator knows what a "sports documentary" has promised before the first slow-motion
    shot.
  </p>

  <h2>Writing film criticism</h2>
  <p>
    Criticism is a genre of writing with its own craft, and its best practitioners are the
    theory's demonstration. The working rules, synthesised from the masters: <strong>describe
    before you judge</strong> (the critique lesson's Feldman order, cinematic edition — quote
    the frame: mise-en-scène analysis is the critic's evidence kit), <strong>take the film's
    own ambitions seriously</strong> (a slow film is not "boring" for being slow — judging
    the film it tried to be is the fairness principle the journalism lesson demands of any
    profile), <strong>argue from the visible</strong> (every aesthetic claim points at
    something on screen; the review is falsifiable in principle), and <strong>write prose
    worth reading without the film</strong> (Kael's conversational voltage, Sontag's
    essayistic range, Farber's termite-art precision, Ebert's plain-spoken analysis — the
    literature lesson's craft applied to argument). The negative review carries the same
    duties: accuracy to the film actually made, and evidence — the difference between a
    pan and a demolition derby. Structurally, criticism also <em>feeds</em> the industry
    (canons are built by critics; "rewatched and revalued" is critical work — Hitchcock's
    elevation from entertainer to auteur was done in print by Cahiers and Sarris), which is
    why the critic's accountability is real: the verdict becomes someone's syllabus.
  </p>

  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "genre is a fixed box the industry imposes on films."
  Genres are negotiated contracts that evolve by repetition and variation: each film cites
  its predecessors, adjusts expectations, and sometimes breaks one clause on purpose — the
  pleasure of a revisionist western comes precisely from the contract it rewrites. The
  studio markets the label; the audience and the film keep renegotiating what it means.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Cognitivism and the post-theory turn.</strong> The 1980s–90s "post-theory"
    movement (Bordwell and Carroll's <em>Post-Theory</em>, 1996) rejected the grand
    psychoanalytic-semiotic apparatus for middle-level research: cognitivism (how do viewers
    actually construct stories, and what does the eye-tracking say about the Kuleshov
    effect?), historical poetics (how have film techniques developed?), and empirical
    audience studies. The clash with apparatus theory remains the discipline's deepest
    methodological divide — meaning built into the machine versus meaning built by minds —
    and both programmes have produced durable results: apparatus theory's ideology analysis,
    cognitivism's measurable comprehension effects (edit comprehension thresholds, continuity
    editing's reliance on attention).
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The spectatorship frontier.</strong> Digital and algorithmic viewing reopens the
    apparatus question with new machinery: the home screen's scroll and speed controls, the
    recommendation engine as an invisible editor of taste (the recommender as apparatus —
    what does the algorithm position the viewer to want?), second-screen attention, and data-
    driven content (films shaped by streaming metrics). Fan studies complicates from the
    other direction: audiences as active communities producing criticism, edits and canon
    pressure (the "release the Snyder cut" campaign as audience power). The theory that
    described a dark room and one projector now describes a personal screen and a feed — and
    the gaze, ideology and contract questions all re-ask themselves at the new scale.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The decolonising debate.</strong> The canon question that reshaped art history is
    reshaping film studies: which films are taught, whose film history is global (the
    syllabus's own structure — global film, documentary, silent movements — is the
    correction institutionalised), how national cinemas are framed outside the Western
    survey, and how preservation resources distribute. Meanwhile the archive itself is being
    revisited (restored "lost" films by women and Black filmmakers — from Alice Guy-Blaché's
    pioneering work to the race films of Oscar Micheaux — rewriting the chronology the old
    canon assumed). Theory, criticism and history converge here, as they have since Cahiers:
    what gets watched, taught and remembered is an argument, and film studies is where the
    argument is argued.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Bazin, What Is Cinema?; Mulvey, "Visual Pleasure and Narrative Cinema" (1975); Bordwell, Post-Theory; the literature lesson's theory chapter.</p>
